WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.420
A useful AI-assisted documentation effort begins with scope.

2
00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:20.280
Define the application, system boundary, environments, business functions, users, roles, external interfaces, internal services, cloud resources, third-party dependencies, and sensitive data types that are in scope.

3
00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:25.980
A vague request such as document the attack surface invites broad, inconsistent output.

4
00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:29.920
A scoped request creates a reviewable work product.

5
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:33.880
Scope should also identify what is not included.

6
00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:42.540
A production application may have development environments, test tenants, old integrations, legacy APIs, and vendor-managed components.

7
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:45.760
Some may be in scope and some may not.

8
00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:52.820
Clear boundaries prevent the AI workflow from blending unrelated systems or implying that unreviewed areas were covered.

9
00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.900
Documentation goals shape the output.

10
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:00.080
Architecture review may need a component summary and trust boundary map.

11
00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:04.980
Threat modeling may need data flows, entry points, and security assumptions.

12
00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:10.040
Compliance may need owners, control evidence, and data classification.

13
00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:15.500
Vulnerability management may need exposed interfaces and dependency context.

14
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.000
Incident readiness may need identity paths, logs, and critical data stores.

15
00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.800
The audience matters.

16
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:27.500
Developers need details that connect to repositories, services, and deployment manifests.

17
00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:33.600
Security teams need exposure, trust boundaries, identity, and risk notes.

18
00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:37.140
Architects need dependency and flow clarity.

19
00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:40.920
Auditors need provenance and review evidence.

20
00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:48.300
Incident responders need owners, critical resources, logging, and recovery context.

21
00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:52.020
A strong scope statement becomes the guardrail for AI use.

22
00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:57.480
It tells the model what evidence to consider, what output to produce, and where to stop.

23
00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:03.020
It also gives human reviewers a way to decide whether the document is complete enough for its purpose.
